WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.047 --> 00:00:07.537
One fine June morning, Miss Polly Harrington hurried into her kitchen.

2
00:00:07.538 --> 00:00:11.972
Nancy, who was busy washing dishes, looked up in surprise.

3
00:00:11.973 --> 00:00:18.348
Although Nancy had worked for Miss Polly for only two months, she'd never seen her employer hurry.

4
00:00:18.349 --> 00:00:22.000
"Nancy," Miss Polly said.

5
00:00:22.001 --> 00:00:28.518
"Yes, ma'am," Nancy answered cheerfully as she continued drying a pitcher.

6
00:00:28.519 --> 00:00:35.334
"When I'm speaking, you should stop working and listen," Miss Polly said in a stern voice.

7
00:00:35.335 --> 00:00:39.629
Nancy set the pitcher on the counter and almost tipped it over.

8
00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:48.695
"Yes, ma'am, I will. I only kept working because you said to hurry with the dishes this morning."

9
00:00:48.696 --> 00:00:56.477
Miss Polly frowned. "I didn't ask for explanations. I asked for your attention."

10
00:00:56.478 --> 00:01:01.011
"Yes, ma'am." Nancy struggled not to sigh.

11
00:01:01.012 --> 00:01:05.908
This was Nancy's first job, and she wondered if there was any way to please Miss Polly.

12
00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:10.071
Nancy had needed to find work when her father died suddenly.

13
00:01:10.072 --> 00:01:16.403
Her sick mother was left with Nancy, who was 16, and 3 younger children.

14
00:01:16.404 --> 00:01:23.008
Before Nancy was hired, she only knew Miss Polly as one of the wealthiest residents of Beldingsville, Vermont.

15
00:01:23.009 --> 00:01:30.587
Now Nancy knew her as a demanding woman who frowned if silverware clattered to the floor or doors banged. Miss

16
00:01:30.588 --> 00:01:35.402
Polly never smiled, even when forks and doors were silent. And

17
00:01:35.403 --> 00:01:40.780
she never gave Nancy a word of praise. "I

18
00:01:40.781 --> 00:01:45.156
want you to clean up the little room in the attic," Miss Polly said. "After

19
00:01:45.157 --> 00:01:51.082
you remove the trunks and boxes, sweep the floor. Then set up the cot." "And

20
00:01:51.083 --> 00:01:54.858
where should I put the things I remove?" "In

21
00:01:54.859 --> 00:01:59.223
the front attic." Miss Polly paused for a moment. "I

22
00:01:59.224 --> 00:02:06.447
suppose I should tell you that my niece, Miss Pollyanna Whittier, is coming to live with me. She's

23
00:02:06.448 --> 00:02:10.498
11 years old, and that will be her room." Nancy's

24
00:02:10.499 --> 00:02:19.724
face broke into a wide grin. "A little girl living here? Oh, that'll be nice!" "Nice?

25
00:02:19.725 --> 00:02:26.652
That isn't exactly the word I'd use." Miss Polly sniffed. "However,

26
00:02:26.653 --> 00:02:30.800
I intend to make the best of the situation. I'm

27
00:02:30.801 --> 00:02:35.063
a good woman, and I know my duty." Nancy

28
00:02:35.064 --> 00:02:41.549
blushed. "Of course, ma'am. I wasn't saying that you weren't good. I

29
00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:50.379
mean . . . I was only thinking that . . . well . . . a child might brighten things up for you." "I

30
00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:56.014
don't see any need for that." Miss Polly abruptly turned to leave the kitchen. "Make

31
00:02:56.015 --> 00:03:00.295
sure you clean the corners of the room," she called over her shoulder. "Yes,

32
00:03:00.296 --> 00:03:03.897
ma'am." Nancy

33
00:03:03.898 --> 00:03:08.638
sighed and went back to drying dishes. ***

34
00:03:08.639 --> 00:03:13.266
A short time later, Miss Polly sat in her room, rereading a letter.

35
00:03:13.267 --> 00:03:18.875
When the letter had arrived from out West, it contained an unpleasant surprise.

36
00:03:18.876 --> 00:03:20.689
Dear Miss Harrington,

37
00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:27.482
I regret to inform you that the Reverend John Whittier has died, making his daughter, Pollyanna, an orphan.

38
00:03:27.483 --> 00:03:31.405
A poor minister, he left nothing except a few books.

39
00:03:31.406 --> 00:03:35.308
I know that Reverend Whittier was your late sister's husband.

40
00:03:35.309 --> 00:03:41.679
Although your families didn't get along, he thought you might want to bring up Pollyanna, for your sister's sake.

41
00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:47.744
A local couple is traveling to Boston this week, so they can accompany Pollyanna for most of the journey.

42
00:03:47.745 --> 00:03:55.444
In Boston, they'll put Pollyanna on a train to Beldingsville. I'll let you know what train she is on.

43
00:03:55.445 --> 00:03:57.869
I hope to hear from you soon. Sincerely,

44
00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:58.998
Jeremiah

45
00:03:58.999 --> 00:04:01.324
White Miss

46
00:04:01.325 --> 00:04:06.798
Polly shook her head as she remembered her sister Jennie, Pollyanna's mother. When

47
00:04:06.799 --> 00:04:11.900
Jennie was twenty, she'd married Whittier against the wishes of her parents. They'd

48
00:04:11.901 --> 00:04:19.049
wanted her to marry a wealthy, older man, but she'd chosen the minister and moved far away. Miss

49
00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:23.308
Polly was 15 when her oldest sister married. For

50
00:04:23.309 --> 00:04:28.312
a while Jennie wrote to her family, although they never replied. All

51
00:04:28.313 --> 00:04:32.632
Jennie's babies died except Pollyanna. Jennie

52
00:04:32.633 --> 00:04:38.298
named the baby for her two sisters, Polly and Anna. A

53
00:04:38.299 --> 00:04:44.335
few years later, Whittier wrote to say Jennie had died. Miss

54
00:04:44.336 --> 00:04:48.399
Polly was forty now and alone in the world. Her

55
00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.420
parents and two sisters had all died. Some

56
00:04:52.421 --> 00:04:56.963
people pitied her lonely life and urged her to have a friend live with her. But

57
00:04:56.964 --> 00:05:01.510
Miss Polly welcomed neither their sympathy nor their advice. She

58
00:05:01.511 --> 00:05:06.635
wasn't lonely; she liked being by herself and preferred quiet. Miss

59
00:05:06.636 --> 00:05:10.751
Polly stood up and put away the letter. "I'll

60
00:05:10.752 --> 00:05:21.509
do my duty and take in Jennie's daughter. But Pollyanna —what a ridiculous name!"

